notes.text:
When you analyse the image, consider:

• **Who** is shown (age, gender, clothing, body language, facial expression) and whether they **look directly at the viewer** (direct gaze creates a personal connection);
• **camera angle and shot**: a **low-angle** shot (looking up) makes the subject seem powerful, heroic or dominant; a **high-angle** shot (looking down) makes it seem small or vulnerable; a **close-up** shows detail or emotion; a **long shot** shows the setting;
• **setting and props**: a mountain suggests adventure, a kitchen suggests family;
• **symbols**: a padlock for security, a heart for love, a leaf for natural ingredients;
• **lighting**: bright light suggests optimism; dark, moody light suggests mystery or luxury.

The **visual** elements (images, colour, layout) and the **verbal** elements (headline, copy, slogan) work together. The words **anchor** the meaning of the image, telling us how to read it, and the image **illustrates** or reinforces the words. Many exam questions ask how the two are linked.
